    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow mono">Why tpo</span>
        <h2>The membrane on most of the roofs you drive past.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="tpo-grid">
        <div class="tpo-copy">
          <p><strong style="color:#fff;">TPO — thermoplastic polyolefin —</strong> is a single-ply white membrane that's become the standard for low-slope commercial roofs. Instead of layers of hot tar and gravel, you get one reflective sheet, rolled out and <strong style="color:#fff;">heat-welded</strong> at every seam into a continuous, monolithic surface. There's no seam tape or glue to fail over time — the seam is melted into one piece of plastic.</p>
          <p>Because it's bright white, TPO reflects sun instead of absorbing it, which keeps roof-deck temperatures down and takes real load off your HVAC in a Houston summer. It also resists the UV, ozone, and grease/chemical exposure that breaks down older roofing materials — which matters a lot if you're roofing a restaurant with rooftop exhaust fans running grease-laden air over the membrane all day.</p>
          <p>We install TPO three ways depending on your deck and wind exposure: <strong style="color:#fff;">mechanically attached</strong> (fastened through the insulation into deck), <strong style="color:#fff;">fully adhered</strong> (bonded down for a cleaner, lower-profile finish), or <strong style="color:#fff;">induction-welded</strong> (fastener plates welded under the membrane, no exposed metal). We'll tell you which one your building actually needs — not just the cheapest one to install.</p>
          <div class="spec-row">
            <div class="spec"><b>45–80</b><span>Mil membrane thickness</span></div>
            <div class="spec"><b>20–30</b><span>Year typical lifespan</span></div>
            <div class="spec"><b>100%</b><span>Heat-welded seams</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>

